1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,880
 In this lesson, you'll learn what rules and memories are, how they work, and why there's

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:09,600
 such a powerful part of your AA workflow.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:14,820
 By the end, you'll understand how to guide the agent consistently across your projects

4
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:17,840
 and help it remember what matters most.

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,640
 Let's start with rules.

6
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:25,720
 Rules are like system-level instructions for your source agent and inline edit.

7
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:31,280
 They tell the AI how to behave, what's style to follow, and what preference to keep in mind

8
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:34,240
 when generating or editing code.

9
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:36,960
 Think of them as persistent context.

10
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:42,040
 Away to teach cursor, how you want things done, even after you close the chat.

11
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:45,680
 Now, how do rules actually work?

12
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,200
 Large language models, like the one behind cursor, don't remember context between

13
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,200
 completions.

14
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,640
 That's exactly why rules exist.

15
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:58,120
 They're loaded automatically at the start of each chat or inline edit, giving the model

16
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:01,480
 consistent and reusable context.

17
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:06,240
 When active, your rules appear in the agent sidebar, so you always know which ones

18
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:08,360
 are guiding the AI.

19
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,240
 There are four main types of rules.

20
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:17,240
 Project rules live in your code base under the folder called dot cursor slash rules.

21
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:21,880
 These are version controlled, so they stay with your project.

22
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:26,320
 Other rules are your personal preferences stored globally in cursor settings.

23
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:28,800
 They apply everywhere you code.

24
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:33,720
 Team rules come from your organization's dashboard, where admins can enforce shared standards

25
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:35,920
 for all developers.

26
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:39,080
 And finally, there's agents.md.

27
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:44,600
 A simple markdown file you can use instead of dot cursor slash rules for quick, readable

28
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:47,240
 project instructions.

29
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:50,960
 Let's look a bit deeper at project rules.

30
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:57,040
 By dot cursor slash rules, each rule is stored as a separate file in MDC format.

31
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:01,600
 This format allows you to include both metadata and instructions.

32
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:07,240
 You can define when a rule should apply, always, automatically based on file patterns,

33
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:09,720
 or only when you call it manually.

34
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:14,640
 For example, you might have one rule for your back and folder and another for your front-end

35
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:15,640
 components.

36
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:22,240
 It's a great way to keep your project logic, architecture, and style decisions consistent.

37
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:26,240
 Next, let's talk about user rules.

38
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,320
 User rules are your personal global preferences.

39
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:33,200
 You can set them in cursor settings under the rules section.

40
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:39,760
 For example, you could add something like write concise answers, or use consistent naming

41
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,760
 for functions.

42
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:46,880
 Use rules apply to everything you do in cursor and help you maintain your preferred communication

43
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:50,280
 or coding style across all your projects.

44
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:55,600
 Now, for larger teams, team rules make it easy to keep everyone aligned.

45
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:00,320
 They're created from the cursor dashboard and can be marked as enforced, meaning no one

46
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:02,760
 can disable them locally.

47
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:10,000
 This ensures every teammate follows the same coding style, workflow, or architecture pattern.

48
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,760
 User rules apply everywhere, across all repositories and projects, and they take precedence

49
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:18,640
 over user and project rules.

50
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:24,880
 If you prefer a simpler setup, you can use agents.md in your project route.

51
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:30,880
 It's just a markdown file with plain text instructions, like use TypeScript for all new

52
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:35,440
 files, or prefer functional React components.

53
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:40,920
 It's perfect for smaller projects where you want clear guidance without extra configuration.

54
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:44,440
 Now let's see how to create and manage rules.

55
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:49,400
 You can create a new rule directly from cursor settings under the rules section, or generate

56
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:54,160
 one from chat using the /generate cursor rules command.

57
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:57,120
 Good rules are short, focused, and actionable.

58
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:01,840
 Keep them under a few hundred lines, split larger ones into smaller files, and always include

59
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:06,640
 clear examples, like templates for components or service files.

60
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:11,840
 That way, the AI will naturally follow your standards without needing extra reminders.

61
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:15,200
 Now let's talk about memories.

62
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:19,960
 Memories work like automatic rules created by cursor based on your conversations.

63
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:24,840
 When you tell the agent something like "always use async await in this project", cursor

64
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:28,680
 can remember it, but only if you approve.

65
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:33,920
 These memories are scoped to your current project and help maintain context across sessions,

66
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:37,520
 so the AI doesn't forget your preferences next time.

67
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:40,760
 Here's how memories are created.

68
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:46,600
 Cursor uses a background observer model that watches your chats and looks for useful information.

69
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:52,720
 When it detects something relevant, like a decision, rule, or pattern, it suggests creating

70
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:54,440
 a memory.

71
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:58,520
 You always get to review and approve it before it's saved.

72
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:03,920
 You can also explicitly ask the agent to remember something, and it will store it as a project

73
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,200
 memory for future use.

74
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:10,440
 You can view and manage all your saved memories from cursor settings under rules.

75
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:16,600
 There, you can edit, delete, or disable any of them if you no longer need them.

76
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:19,960
 This gives you full control over what the AI remembers.

77
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:26,080
 So to wrap up, rules define how cursor behaves and memories help it remember what matters

78
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:27,400
 to you.

79
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:33,000
 Together, they make the agent smarter, more consistent, and perfectly tuned to your coding

80
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,120
 style and project needs.

81
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:39,480
 I hope this lesson was useful for you and see you in the next lesson.

